<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">War and march · c. 1706 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The British Grenadiers</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old British marching song, played since the 1700s by soldiers whose badge shows a grenade.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>anonymous</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional (a tune of the early 17th century)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>British soldiers</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Great Britain</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>tune early 17th century; words perhaps 1702–1713</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>War and march</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (anonymous; the words perhaps from the War of the Spanish Succession (1702–1713), quoted as &quot;old&quot; in 1786)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-british-grenadiers">The British Grenadiers</a>&quot; is a traditional marching song of the British and Commonwealth military units whose badge bears a grenade. Its tune is old and its origins are disputed, though they are generally put in the early 17th century: John Playford's collection of dance tunes of 1728 has it as &quot;The New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bath">Bath</a>&quot;, the Victorian musicologist William Chappell linked it to a work of 1622, &quot;Sir Edward Nowell's Delight&quot;, and in 1907 the composer Ernest Walker called the melody &quot;three centuries' evolution of an Elizabethan tune&quot;. It was brought into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a> as a military march in the reign of William III (1689–1702) and resembles one written for Prince John William of Friesland; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-grattan">Henry Grattan</a> Flood proposed another ancestor, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> march &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus</a> van Nassouwe&quot; of 1672, itself reworked from a French version of 1568.</p><p>The song is about grenadiers in general, not the Grenadier Guards, and every fusilier unit was entitled to use it. It is said to commemorate the assault of 700 British grenadiers on the French-held fortress of Namur in August 1695, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nine-years-war-ireland">Nine Years' War</a>. A &quot;Granadeer's March&quot; was mentioned in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> publication of 1706, though it is not certain that it was the same tune, and in 1786 Francis Grose, in his Military Antiquities, quoted two lines of a &quot;grenadier song&quot; he already thought old. The words as sung may go back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> (1702–1713): they speak of grenadiers throwing grenades, of the tall caps and the heavy pouches in which grenades were carried, and of the looped coats, with broad bands of lace across the chest, that marked the early grenadiers. Some of its words have grown obscure: a fusee is the short <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> carried by grenadier officers, the glacis the open slope in front of a fortress's walls, a bumper a cup filled for a toast, and a run of nonsense syllables imitates the drums that kept soldiers in step.</p><p>It became the regimental quick march of the Royal Artillery and the Honourable Artillery Company (both since 1716), of the Grenadier Guards and the Royal Regiment of Fusiliers (since 1763) and of the Corps of Royal Engineers (since 1787), and an authorised march of artillery and grenadier regiments in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>; its standard orchestration for military band was approved in 1762. It was popular in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> throughout the 18th and 19th centuries and is most often heard today at the yearly Trooping the Colour, when the Colour Escort marches onto Horse Guards Parade.</p><p>Its tune has carried many other words. Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolution">American Revolution</a>, Joseph Warren wrote &quot;Free America&quot; to it in 1774, and the Irish poet Edward Lysaght, a supporter of the Irish Volunteers, set his ballad &quot;The Man Who Led the Van of the Irish Volunteers&quot;, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-grattan">Henry Grattan</a>, to the English melody. In 1871 women students of Girton College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, sang &quot;The Girton Pioneers&quot; to it in honour of the first three women to pass the university's tripos examinations, and it has also been set to church hymns. Ignaz Moscheles made it the main theme of the finale of his Piano Concerto No. 4 (1823) and Charles Ives used it in &quot;Putnam's Camp&quot;, the second movement of Three Places in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-england">New England</a>. Like &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rule-britannia">Rule, Britannia!</a>&quot;, it is often heard in films and on television to mark a British setting or character.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A soldiers' boast. The song names the heroes of the ancient world and then claims that none of them can match the British grenadiers, who march up to the enemy's walls and throw their grenades under fire, and it ends with a toast to them. The mood is swaggering and cheerful, carried by the beat of the drum.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Some talk of Alexander,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>and some of Hercules,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Of Hector and Lysander,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>and such great men as these;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But all the world acknowledges,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>true valour best appears.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With a tow, row, row, row, row, row,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>brave British Grenadiers</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>These ancients of antiquity,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>ne'er saw a cannon ball;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Nor knew the force of powder,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>to stay their foes with all:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But braver boys have known its</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>and banish'd all their fears,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For a tow, row, row, row, row, row,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>brave British Grenadeers.</span></p></div><details class="sg-more"><summary>Show all 56 lines</summary><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When we receive the orders,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>to storm their palisadoes;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Our leaders march with fuzees,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>and we with hand granadoes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>We toss them from the glacis,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>amongst our en'mies ears,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With a tow, row, row, row, row, row,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>brave British Grenadiers.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Then Jove the God of thunder,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>and Mars the God of war;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Rough Neptune with his Trident,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Apollo in his Car;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And all the Gods celestial,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>descending from their spheres,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Do behold with admiration</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>brave British Grenadiers.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But be you Whig or Tory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>or any other thing,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I'd have you still remember,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>t'obey Great George our King,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>For if you prove rebellious,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>we'll thunder in your ears,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With a tow, row. row, row, row, row,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>brave British Grenadiers.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And when the siege is over,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>we to the town repairs,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The Citizens cry, huzza, boys,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>here come the Grenadiers:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Here come the Grenadiers boys,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>without e'er dread or fear,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With a tow, row, row, row, row, row,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>brave British Grenadiers.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Come fill us up a bumper,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>and let us drink to those,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who carry Caps and Pouches,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>and wear the laced clothes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>May they and their commanders,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>live happy many years,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>With a tow, row row, row, row, row,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>brave British Grenadiers.</span></p></div></details></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: a chapbook printed in Glasgow (1802). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_British_Grenadiers_(1802)%2FThe_British_Grenadiers" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
