<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Free America (Joseph Warren)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1774</span><span class="sg-chain-h">parody to the same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Man Who Led the Van of the Irish Volunteers (Edward Lysaght)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">ballad to the same tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Piano Concerto No. 4 (Ignaz Moscheles)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1823</span><span class="sg-chain-h">main theme of the finale</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Girton Pioneers</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1871</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words to the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Putnam's Camp, Redding, Connecticut (Charles Ives)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1912</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the melody plays a central role in the movement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Arabi</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the first verse of the Basel Carnival march</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">In Praise of E. M. P. (Harold Baum)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">words on glycolysis to the tune</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The New Bath</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1728</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune in John Playford's collection</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Sir Edward Nowell's Delight</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1622</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a possible ancestor of the tune (William Chappell)</span></li><li><a href="/songs/wilhelmus">Wilhelmus van Nassouwe</a> <span class="sg-chain-y">1672</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a possible ancestor of the tune (Henry Grattan Flood)</span></li></ul></section>
